  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Haiti Cholera Response</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">- Raising awareness among the population.
- Promoting individual and collective hygiene habits to stop the spread of the epidemic.
- Improving the population&apos;s access to drinking water.
- Increasing the population&apos;s knowledge of water disinfection and the proper use of latrines and other sanitary facilities.
- Strengthening the capacity of the Haitian Red Cross&apos;s regional and local committees to manage the response.
- Strengthening the position of the Haitian Red Cross as an auxiliary to the public authorities.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Contribution to the Haitian Red Cross lead response to Cholera through the national Response Plan. NLRC Supports the national plan in PaP and the regional plan in the Sud-Est and Grand-Anse region.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Port-au-Prince, Grand-Anse and Sud-Est</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The first cholera cases were reported in Port Au Prince on the 2nd of October. Due to the increased insecurity, mobility, protests, and fuel shortages, access to clean drinking water and proper sanitation is limited. This is increasing the risks of spreading. Access to water, adequate sanitation, and hygiene are essential to containing the spread of the disease at the country level. The limited access to affected areas hinders surveillance and assessment of the cases.</narrative>
  </description>
